A SaaS platform implements a Multiton pattern (a generalized Singleton keyed by tenant ID) to manage per-tenant configuration objects. The pattern uses a ConcurrentHashMap<TenantId, Config> as the registry. Under production load, engineers observe that two Config instances are occasionally created for the same tenant during peak onboarding traffic.

Given this code path: if (!map.containsKey(id)) { map.put(id, new Config(id)); } return map.get(id);

What is the correct fix, and why is the simpler computeIfAbsent(id, Config::new) not always safe in this context?

Your engineering team has inherited a high-throughput, low-latency ad-bidding engine written in C++11. The system features a highly optimized cache manager that is instantiated exactly once. The original developers implemented this using the classic Singleton pattern with Double-Checked Locking (DCL) to ensure thread safety during lazy initialization without the overhead of acquiring a lock on every access.

The code looks like this:
cpp
CacheManager* CacheManager::instance = nullptr;
std::mutex CacheManager::mutex_;

CacheManager* CacheManager::getInstance() {
    if (instance == nullptr) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        if (instance == nullptr) {
            instance = new CacheManager();
        }
    }
    return instance;
}


The system runs flawlessly on x86 architectures. However, the company recently migrated the bidding engine to a cluster of custom ARM-based processors to reduce cloud compute costs. Immediately after the migration, the application begins experiencing intermittent segmentation faults and undefined behavior. Diagnostic core dumps reveal that threads are occasionally accessing a partially constructed CacheManager object.

As the software architect, you are tasked with identifying the exact mechanism of this failure mode and prescribing the standard, modern creational resolution.

Why did the Double-Checked Locking Singleton fail specifically on the ARM architecture, and what is the most appropriate C++11 standard-compliant creational solution to fix it while maintaining lazy initialization?

You are auditing a high-security cryptography module written in Java. The module requires exactly one instance of an expensive SecureRandomEntropyGenerator. The developers, aware of the pitfalls of Double-Checked Locking (DCL) prior to Java 1.5, opted for the Initialization-on-Demand Holder idiom (also known as the Bill Pugh Singleton pattern). 

The code is structured as follows:
java
public class EntropyGenerator {
    private EntropyGenerator() { /* Expensive OS-level initialization */ }
    private static class LazyHolder {
        static final EntropyGenerator INSTANCE = new EntropyGenerator();
    }
    public static EntropyGenerator getInstance() {
        return LazyHolder.INSTANCE;
    }
}


The security compliance team has raised a concern. They argue that under extreme concurrent load, or if the Java Virtual Machine's Just-In-Time (JIT) compiler performs aggressive instruction reordering, multiple threads might bypass the initialization lock and see a partially constructed EntropyGenerator object, compromising the cryptographic integrity.

As the lead software architect, evaluate the compliance team's claim. Does the Initialization-on-Demand Holder idiom guarantee safe publication and thread safety without explicit synchronization, and if so, what specific mechanism of the JVM specification enforces this creational guarantee?
